{"text":"I really like this outline. I'm going to make a couple of points. Can you update the outline to incorporate some of these? For 5.1, I think that's fine. 5.2. I would say that one potential caveat to this sensitivity trade-off is that to some extent because our data set is very imbalanced it doesn't really it doesn't necessarily set us up for like good sensitivity specificity if we had a balanced data set it might be easier to try to like nudge the model towards getting the best of both worlds but that is a speculative argument and I'm not entirely sure if it's correct so you can let me know if you think I'm being dumb there for patient specific fine-tuning yeah I like the points you're making the only thing I would add there is that like potentially like the way we selected points was just the way we selected examples for the PSFT was random there might be a more sophisticated way to select examples that are a bit more representative of what's actually happening for that patient and it's possible that by selecting a better set of sample pulses you might be able to produce better results but again speculation because I haven't spent the time to go like painstakingly look at the specific waveforms that I was using and yeah leave the leave 5.3 as is for now I'll come back to that once I've actually done that analysis and I think the limitations look good I'll once I'm writing this I'll find some more papers to sprinkle in there"}